# Feature-Sliced Design in this project

> Generated by `sveltekit-fsd init`. Edit freely — this file is the convention,
> not a lock file. Keep it in step with the code; a doc describing the previous
> shape is worse than no doc.

## Layout

```text
{{srcDir}}/
├── app/                     # FSD app layer — and SvelteKit's own entry points
│   ├── index.html           # appTemplate (was {{srcDir}}/app.html)
│   ├── routes/              # SvelteKit routing — thin, see below
│   ├── layouts/             # shared route shells
│   ├── providers/           # context providers, mounted once in +layout.svelte
│   └── styles/              # the project-wide stylesheet and theme
├── pages/<page>/            # one slice per route: ui/, index.ts (public API)
├── features/<slice>/        # a whole user action, once two pages need it
├── entities/<slice>/        # a business object, once two features need it
└── shared/                  # infrastructure only — api/, auth/, config/, lib/, ui/
```

Routing lives **inside** the app layer, which is what the
[FSD SvelteKit guide](https://feature-sliced.design/docs/guides/tech/with-sveltekit)
asks for and the opposite of where SvelteKit puts it by default. Three settings
make that real, and they live in the SvelteKit config (`vite.config.ts` in a
project made by a recent `sv create`, `svelte.config.js` in an older one):

```js
files: {
  routes: '{{routesDir}}',
  lib: '{{srcDir}}',
  appTemplate: '{{srcDir}}/app/index.html'
},
alias: {
  '{{alias}}/*': '{{srcDir}}/*'
}
```

`lib: '{{srcDir}}'` is the one worth understanding. It points `$lib` at the same
tree `{{alias}}` points at, so `$lib/shared/ui` and `{{alias}}/shared/ui` are the
same module. Without it, `{{srcDir}}/lib/` survives as a second home for shared
code next to `shared/` — which is the exact drift FSD exists to prevent. The
import boundary knows both spellings anyway, because a rule with a documented
bypass is not a rule.

`init` does not touch `tsconfig.json`: SvelteKit writes the alias into the
generated `.svelte-kit/tsconfig.json` that yours extends, so `svelte-check`,
your editor and steiger all resolve `{{alias}}/` from the same place Vite does.
`static/` is left where SvelteKit put it — the FSD guide renames it to `public/`,
which is a rename with no FSD content, and a rename is not free.

**Add layers when a second consumer actually appears, not up front.**
Duplicating a component across two pages is cheaper to undo than an `entities/`
layer built for a reuse that never happens.

`widgets/` is left closed here, and that is **this project's** call rather than
the spec's. FSD v2.1 has it as an ordinary layer; its only caution is that a UI
block which is most of a page's content and is never reused should not be one.
The reasoning for closing it: a reusable block with its own fetching and state
is already a feature, so the widget/feature boundary would be one more decision
per screen buying nothing. Reach for `features/` first. Reopen it by deleting
this paragraph, not by asking the spec's permission.

When the second consumer does appear and what it shares is *data* — the types
and query hooks for one domain — `shared/<domain>/` is usually the honest home:
`shared/auth`, one file per capability. It bends "shared is infrastructure" a
little, and it buys not paying for a layer whose whole promise is a third
consumer that may never arrive. A `features/` slice is for a user *action* two
pages perform, which is a different thing.

**Two signals say that advice has run out.** Neither is a page count — a
`shared/` segment read by three pages can be perfectly correct.

The first is a **business rule landing in the segment**. Types and query hooks
are plumbing; a calculation, a format or a constraint that is *about* the
business is not, and `shared/` is defined as the layer with no business meaning.
When one shows up, the segment has become an entity and is only filed under
`shared/`.

The second is more expensive and quieter: **two `pages` slices each writing
their own copy** of the same API resource. They cannot import each other, so
nothing makes the two agree — not review, not the compiler. Expect the type in
one to be missing fields the other has, both to sit on the same cache key with
one of them writing it as a bare string, and each to invalidate its own list and
not the other's. That is not the cheap duplication this document argues for
elsewhere; the cheap kind is one a compiler or a reader can see. Move it below
both, and let the question of which lower layer be settled by the paragraph
above.

The full methodology is in the `feature-sliced-design` skill `init` wrote at
`.agents/skills/feature-sliced-design/` — layer definitions, the `@x`
cross-import pattern, when an entity earns its keep, and asset placement. This
file is only how it lands in *this* repo.

## Import boundary

Imports point downwards only: `app → pages → widgets → features → entities →
shared`. Two slices on the same layer never import each other, and a slice is
always imported through its `index.ts`:

```ts
import { LoginPage } from "{{alias}}/pages/login";                 // ✅ public API
import LoginForm from "{{alias}}/pages/login/ui/login-form.svelte"; // ❌ reaches inside
```

If two pages need the same thing, that is the signal it was never page-scoped:
move it down a layer instead of importing sideways. Two copies in two pages while
you are still unsure is the cheaper mistake.

## Two linters, on purpose

`{{lintCommand}}` runs both, and they are not redundant:

| | catches | when |
|---|---|---|
| **ESLint** (`eslint.fsd.js`) | this import points the wrong way, or reaches past a slice's `index.ts` | as you type, per file, in the editor |
| **steiger** (`steiger.config.ts`) | the same imports, plus a slice with no references, a layer sliced too finely, a segment named after its type, inconsistent naming | on demand, whole tree |

The overlap on imports is deliberate, not an oversight to clean up. steiger's
`fsd/forbidden-imports`, `fsd/no-cross-imports` and `fsd/no-public-api-sidestep`
do check the boundary — ESLint is not covering a gap in the plugin. It is
covering a gap in *timing*: steiger walks the tree when you run it, and a rule
you only meet in CI is a rule you have already built ten files on top of. Do not
delete the ESLint half on the grounds that steiger repeats it.

The other direction has no overlap at all: nothing in one file can show that a
slice has no consumers or that a layer has thirty slices, so that half only works
whole-tree.

`{{routesDir}}/` is in steiger's `ignores`, and that is not it being excused. It
is a SvelteKit segment whose every file is called `+page.svelte`; steiger would
read that tree as slices and report on names SvelteKit chose. Its import boundary
is still enforced — by ESLint, which is the half that can see an import at all.

`eslint.fsd.js` has no dependencies of its own: the boundary is expressed with
the core `no-restricted-imports` rule, and the layer order above is the whole of
it. One gotcha if you edit it — flat config **replaces** a rule's options when a
later block matches the same file instead of merging them, so every pattern for a
layer has to stay in that layer's single block. The `{{routesDir}}/` block is the
live example: it sits inside `app/`, so it comes after the `app` block and
repeats the patterns that still apply. Split them across two blocks and the
earlier one silently stops applying.

When steiger says *"this slice has only one reference — consider merging them"*,
that is the "add layers when a second consumer appears" rule talking, not a false
positive. It is configured as a **warning** rather than an error, because every
slice has one consumer on the day it is created and failing the build for that
teaches people to delete the rule instead of the slice. Read it anyway: a slice
that stays at one consumer for good probably belongs inside that consumer.

## `{{routesDir}}/` stays thin

A route file renders an FSD page and nothing else:

```svelte
<!-- {{routesDir}}/login/+page.svelte -->
<script lang="ts">
	import { LoginPage } from "{{alias}}/pages/login";
</script>

<LoginPage />
```

There is no `metadata` to forward: a Svelte page carries its own `<svelte:head>`,
so the title travels with the component. A `+page.ts` or `+page.server.ts` beside
it is the framework's business — `load` belongs there, the shape it returns
belongs in a slice.

## Segments

A slice gets only the segments it has code for. `ui/` alone is the common case;
add `model/` (state), `api/` (requests) or `lib/` (helpers) when that slice
actually has them. An empty segment folder is noise.

Adding one later is the normal path, not a rewrite — re-run the same command with
the extra segment and it writes only what is missing, appending the new exports
to the slice's `index.ts` and leaving every existing file untouched:

```bash
sveltekit-fsd generate slice features checkout --segments ui        # ui/ only
sveltekit-fsd generate slice features checkout --segments ui,model  # adds model/
```

```text
{{srcDir}}/pages/<page>/
├── ui/<page>-page.svelte     # the page, with its own <svelte:head>
├── model/<page>.svelte.ts    # runes — see the filename rule below
└── index.ts                  # public API — the only thing a route imports
```

**A module that uses runes must be named `*.svelte.ts`.** `$state` and `$derived`
are compiled, not imported, and the Svelte compiler only looks inside `.svelte`
components and modules named that way. Rename such a file to a plain `.ts` and
`$state` becomes an undefined function at runtime, with nothing failing at build
time to say so. The import that reaches it drops only the `.ts`:
`import { useThing } from "./model/thing.svelte"`.

## Where state lives

Reactive state a slice owns goes in `model/<name>.svelte.ts`, behind a function
that returns getters — exporting a `$state` variable directly exports the value
it had at module load, and nothing ever updates.

Anything that comes from the API is not state, it is a cache: that belongs in
`api/` as a TanStack Query hook. The two mistakes worth naming are copying server
data into `$state` (now there are two truths and one of them is stale) and
reaching for a store where a query already answers.

`createQuery`/`createMutation` and `useQueryClient` must be called during
component initialisation — from a `<script>` block, or from a function a
`<script>` block calls. They read Svelte's context, which exists nowhere else. An
event handler calling one throws, and the message does not say why.

## Error copy, and i18n if you add one

Every failure a user reads is resolved from a catalog — `Record<code, string>`,
owned by the domain that raises those codes — and never from `error.message`,
which is a backend detail written for a log.

The generated sentences are a first draft in one language (`sveltekit-fsd config
show` says which). That is all `locale` decides: there is no runtime i18n here,
and a project that adds one does not need a different shape from the CLI. A
catalog is a plain object, so build it from the translator where it is rendered:

```svelte
<FormError error={save.error} catalogs={[{ CONFLICT: t("conflict") }]} fallback={t("saveFailed")} />
```

The codes stay the keys. They are the API's contract, not copy, so they do not
translate.

{{#if (eq locale "th")}}
## Thai copy renders differently

Three things Latin-tuned defaults get wrong. All three are invisible until
someone reads the screen in Thai:

- **Never put negative tracking on Thai.** `tracking-tight` and friends collapse
  tone marks into the vowels beneath them, and Thai does not letterspace for
  emphasis either, so a wide `tracking-*` reads as broken rather than as a label.
- **Nothing Thai goes inside a `font-mono` element.** A mono face loaded with the
  latin subset has no Thai glyph at all, so the string drops to a system fallback
  mid-word. Split the branch instead of wrapping both sides. Mono marks a value
  as literal — an id, a code, an account number — not prose, and not a column of
  numbers you only want lined up.
- **Line heights need room per text size.** Thai stacks up to two marks above a
  consonant plus a vowel below, so Tailwind's defaults (1.2 at `text-3xl`) drop
  one line's descenders into the next line's marks. Override
  `--text-*--line-height` for every size the theme actually uses.

{{/if}}
## Forms, and inline or toast

One question with one answer: does the user have to **fix** this, or only
**know** it?

- Something they must fix stays inline — every form error, and every failure that
  replaces a screen. `<FormError>` is that half. A toast is gone in seconds and
  points at no field, which is the wrong home for "your password is wrong" while
  someone is still typing.
- Something they only need to know goes to a toast — the result of a row button,
  a checkbox in a matrix, anything whose banner would otherwise sit far from the
  thing acted on.

A toast helper takes a **finished sentence**, never an error. Resolving a code
into copy belongs to the domain that raises it — the
`resolve<Domain>Error(error, fallback)` beside its catalog — so the toast layer
never learns that catalogs exist. Do not replace either half with a
QueryClient-wide `mutationCache.onError`: one handler cannot name the action that
failed, and it fires over the dialog errors that have to stay inline.

Forms get **one helper, not per-field state written again in every form**:
validate on submit, keep the messages in one place, and let the field component
own the `aria-invalid` / `aria-describedby` wiring. Client validation is a
courtesy — the API re-validates regardless — so name the server-side rule each
field mirrors in a comment, and a drift costs a round trip rather than
correctness.

## Generating

```bash
sveltekit-fsd generate page <name>            # slice + {{routesDir}}/<name>/+page.svelte
sveltekit-fsd generate slice features <name>  # a feature slice
sveltekit-fsd generate layout admin           # app/layouts + {{routesDir}}/(admin)/+layout.svelte
sveltekit-fsd add error-handling              # shared/api: ApiError, catalogs, client
sveltekit-fsd add auth                        # shared/auth: session, guard, login page
```

Re-running a generate command extends what is there instead of refusing, so
`--errors` or `--model` on a page built bare earlier just adds the file. It never
rewrites one you already have — and it will not add a second route file for a
page that is already routed from somewhere else.

Layouts live in `app/layouts`, not `pages`: a layout is not one route's content,
it is what several routes have in common, and
`{{routesDir}}/(group)/+layout.svelte` renders it the same way a route file
renders a page. `shadcn-svelte add <name>` writes into `{{srcDir}}/shared/ui` —
`components.json` is already aimed there.
